+## Results — Muse Spark 1.3, 10 defects × 3 systems × 3 repeats (2026-10-27)
+
+Run `20261027-101729-meta` ([JSON](../benchmarks/results/20261027-101729-meta.json),
+[HTML](../benchmarks/results/20261027-101729-meta.html)): `muse-spark-1.3`, standard mode,
+`reasoning_effort=medium`, 8,192 max tokens, temperature 0.2. 90 investigations, 0 failed model
+calls, **$2.89** spent in total.
+
+| Metric | Baseline A · one-shot | Baseline C · edit-test loop | FORGE-0 |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Verified repair rate (hidden-test confirmed) | 93% (28/30) | **100% (30/30)** | **100% (30/30)** |
+| False-positive repair claims | **2** (D6 ×2) | 0 | 0 |
+| Claimed fixes with regressions | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+| Mean experiments per investigation | 0 | 0 | 3.9 |
+| Model calls | 30 | 32 | 98 |
+| Tokens | 222,241 | 240,001 | 1,015,274 |
+| Mean wall time per investigation | 23 s | 25 s | 63 s |
+| Cost (list price) | $0.37 · $0.012 per run | $0.40 · $0.013 per run | $2.12 · $0.071 per run |
+
+Per defect, every system fixed D1–D5, D7–D9 and R1 in all three repeats. The differences are all
+on **D6**, the two-cause defect:
+
+* **Baseline A** fixed the refund amount but not the restock race in two of three runs. The target
+  test still failed, the verifier rejected the patch, and a one-shot tool would have shipped it
+  anyway: those are its two false positives.
+* **Baseline C** needed a second attempt in two of three runs: its first patch fixed one cause, the
+  test run showed the target still failing, and the retry fixed the other.
+* **FORGE-0** ran 3–7 experiments that separated the two causes, and its first patch was verified
+  in two of three runs (the third needed one revision).
+
+**What this shows, honestly.** With a model as strong as Muse Spark 1.3, any system that *runs the
+tests and retries* matches FORGE-0's fix rate on this set, at about a fifth of the cost; only the
+one-shot baseline makes false claims. On these defects FORGE-0's advantage is not a higher fix rate.
+It is that every fix arrives with its evidence: the explanations that were considered and ruled
+out, the experiments that ruled them out, the flaky-test classification, and an independent
+verification. That is what turns a patch into a pull request a reviewer can trust, which is what
+`forge0-ci` uses it for. A fix-rate gap would need harder defects than these ten.
+
+**Reproduction.** The run recorded a 90% reproduction rate: D9's nine investigations counted as
+not reproduced, because the metric then looked only at *isolated* re-runs, and D9 by definition
+passes alone. The whole-file re-run did reproduce it, and the triage classified it as
+order-dependent. The metric now counts that re-run (fixed after this run, checked on D9 in the real
+sandbox), which makes the rate 100% for this dataset.
+
+## Pre-registered harder defects and an open-weights arm (2026-10-27)
+
+Because the ten defects did not separate the loop from FORGE-0, three more (D10–D12, whose root
+cause is a runtime fact a local patch can hide) and two arms were
+[pre-registered](../benchmarks/preregistration/2026-10-27-harder-defects.md) and committed before
+any run, together with predictions and the rule for claiming that FORGE-0 beats the loop.
+
+| Arm | Baseline A · one-shot | Baseline C · loop | FORGE-0 |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| **A.** Muse Spark 1.3, D10–D12 × 3 (`20261027-115421-meta`, $1.02) | 9/9 · 0 false claims | 9/9 · 0 | 9/9 · 0 |
+| **B.** Muse Spark Open only, all 13 defects × 3 (`20261027-132544-openai`, $6.06) | not run | **38/39** · 1 false claim | 30/39 · **0** |
+
+* **Arm A is a tie.** The predictions that the loop would often miss D10 and D11 were wrong: every
+  system, the one-shot baseline included, fixed the whole cause rather than the local symptom.
+* **Arm B goes to the loop.** Seven of FORGE-0's nine misses are runs that never got a plan: Muse
+  Spark Open spent its whole 16,384-token budget reasoning in the experiment-design call and
+  returned nothing usable. The other two are D11 ending inconclusive. On the 32 runs where every
+  call returned, FORGE-0 fixed 30. The loop's one miss is a false claim (D1 #1 passed the visible
+  tests and failed the hidden ones); FORGE-0 made none. FORGE-0 cost $0.135 per run, the loop
+  $0.020.
+
+So on everything measured so far, **FORGE-0 does not beat an edit-test loop on fix rate**. With an
+open-weights model it loses, mainly because its experiment-design step is fragile on that model.
+Its consistent advantage is precision: in the three benchmarks of the current code (78 FORGE-0
+runs), no fix FORGE-0 claimed failed the hidden tests, while the one-shot baseline made 2 false
+claims in 39 runs and the loop 1 in 78. Making experiment design robust on open models (a smaller plan, a lower reasoning effort
+on retry, or falling back to a direct repair) is the next change, and per the pre-registration it
+will be reported as a new run on all defects.
+
